      "text": "To find \\( f(2) \\), we start with the given functional equation:\n\\[ f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x. \\]\n\nFirst, let's substitute \\( x = 2 \\) into the equation:\n\\[ f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 4^2 = 16. \\]\nThis gives us the first equation:\n\\[ f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 16. \\tag{1} \\]\n\nNext, let's substitute \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\) into the original equation:\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 4^{\\frac{1}{2}} = 2. \\]\nThis gives us the second equation:\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 2. \\tag{2} \\]\n\nWe now have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\( f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 16, \\)\n2. \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 2. \\)\n\nTo eliminate \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\), we can multiply the first equation by 2 and then add it to the second equation:\n\\[ 2 f(2) - 4 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 32, \\]\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 2. \\]\nAdding these equations, we get:\n\\[ (2 f(2) - 4 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)) + (f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2)) = 32 + 2, \\]\n\\[ -3 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 34, \\]\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = -\\frac{34}{3}. \\]\n\nNow substitute \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = -\\frac{34}{3} \\) back into equation (1):\n\\[ f(2) - 2 \\left( -\\frac{34}{3} \\right) = 16, \\]\n\\[ f(2) + \\frac{68}{3} = 16, \\]\n\\[ f(2) = 16 - \\frac{68}{3}, \\]\n\\[ f(2) = \\frac{48}{3} - \\frac{68}{3}, \\]\n\\[ f(2) = -\\frac{20}{3}. \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( f(2) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-\\frac{20}{3}}\\).",
